{"type":"message","id":"a3e54b2a","parentId":"5cb71bac","timestamp":"2026-05-27T08:20:13.359Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"So the code changes were committed in round 1. Now I need to address what the evaluator flagged. Looking at the evaluator feedback: \"No code was changed in the allowed files\" - this was the feedback for round 2. Round 1 already made the changes but was rejected for something else, and round 2 apparently didn't change anything.\n\nLet me check what the `unused_fragment.rs` concern is. The plan says to replace `FRAGMENT_INCLUDE_RE` with the parser, but the production syntax used is `{{> name }}` while the parser handles `{> name <}`. The existing tests already pass. Let me check if `unused_fragment.rs` update is needed by the plan and what the correct behavior should be.\n\nLooking at the plan:\n> 2. In `unused_fragment.rs`, replace the `FRAGMENT_INCLUDE_RE` regex with the parser to detect fragment inclusions.\n\nBut the parser's `{> name <}` and production `{{> name }}` are different syntaxes. If the plan says to use the parser for fragment detection, maybe the production format is `{> name <}` and the existing test `{{> note }}` is outdated.\n\nLet me check the compile.rs to see which syntax is actually processed:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_01RdhFkyj6qrquH32S1dhdzu","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"fragment\\|{>\" X:/repo/skillet/.worktrees/goal-move-the-lint-pipelines-p-mpnrlu69-qmv6ab/crates/skillet/src/compile.rs | head -20"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"github-copilot","model":"claude-sonnet-4.6","usage":{"input":1,"output":395,"cacheRead":38820,"cacheWrite":590,"totalTokens":39806,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779870000573,"responseId":"msg_bdrk_0188Nwg89dz9U5nVa1QxWcJ3"}}
{"type":"message","id":"45bb0e5f","parentId":"a3e54b2a","timestamp":"2026-05-27T08:20:13.639Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_bdrk_01RdhFkyj6qrquH32S1dhdzu","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"24:    LazyLock::new(|| Regex::new(r\"^\\{\\{>\\s*([\\w-]+)\\s*\\}\\}\\s*$\").unwrap());\n44:    /// Loaded fragment content keyed by fragment name (without `.fragment.pan`).\n45:    pub fragments: HashMap<String, String>,\n70:    pub fragments_used: Vec<String>,\n182:/// (missing fragment, undefined var/env ref, missing file ref, or frontmatter\n196:    let (processed_body, fragments_used) = process_fragments(&body, &ctx.fragments)?;\n225:        fragments_used,\n298:/// Expands `{{> fragment-name }}` include directives in `body`.\n300:/// Returns the expanded body and the list of fragment names used.\n301:fn process_fragments(\n303:    fragments: &HashMap<String, String>,\n305:    let mut fragments_used: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n313:            let content = fragments\n315:                .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!(\"fragment '{}' not found\", frag_name))?;\n316:            // Reject nested includes — keep fragment includes flat (v1 decision).\n320:                        \"fragment '{}' contains a nested fragment include on line {} — \\\n327:            if !fragments_used.iter().any(|f| f == frag_name) {\n328:                fragments_used.push(frag_name.to_string());\n336:    Ok((out_lines.join(\"\\n\"), fragments_used))\n556:    let fragments_dir = workspace.join(&cfg.workspace.fragments_dir);\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779870013639}}
{"type":"message","id":"ca44cc96","parentId":"45bb0e5f","timestamp":"2026-05-27T08:20:40.682Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The compile.rs uses `{{> name }}` syntax (line 24), not `{> name <}`. The parser uses `{> name <}`. So:\n1. Production syntax for fragment inclusion is `{{> name }}`\n2. Parser handles `{> name <}` syntax\n\nThese are two different syntaxes. The plan to replace `unused_fragment.rs` regex with the parser doesn't make sense if the production syntax is `{{> name }}`.\n\nHowever, the plan is the canonical document and says to use the parser. But the evaluator feedback says \"All existing tests must continue to pass.\" If I switch `unused_fragment.rs` to use the parser, the test `check_unused_fragments_silent_when_fragment_is_used` which uses `{{> note }}` would break.\n\n\nLooking at the evaluator feedback again, they're saying both ref extraction and fragment parsing need to be implemented. I see that `refs.rs` has already been updated, but `unused_fragment.rs` still uses the regex. The problem is that if the parser syntax `{> name <}` doesn't match what production uses (`{{> name }}`), switching to the parser would break existing tests. Let me check the test at line 644 more carefully—it uses `{{> note }}`, and the existing regex pattern matches this format.\n\nIf I switch `unused_fragment.rs` to use the parser for `{> name <}` syntax, the test using `{{> note }}` wouldn't match anymore. Since the acceptance criteria requires all existing tests to pass, I'd need to update the test alongside any changes to the rule. But looking at the acceptance tests listed, none specifically test `unused_fragment`, so I need to figure out whether updating the test is actually part of the requirements. Hold on - I'm checking the allowed files list against what I'm supposed to modify. The `unused_fragment.rs` file isn't actually in the allowed files section, only `refs.rs`, `pipeline.rs`, and the compiler files are listed